    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "The piano is a keyboard chordophone where hammers strike strings. 88 keys (standard) span over seven octaves in equal temperament. Invented c. 1700 by Bartolomeo Cristofori in Italy."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism",
      "title": "Mechanism",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Key depression launches felt hammer; damper stops vibration on release. Sustain pedal lifts all dampers. Cast-iron frame (19th century) withstands 20+ tons string tension."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "types",
      "title": "Types",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Grand piano: horizontal strings, superior control — concert halls. Upright: vertical strings, domestic and school use. Fortepiano (historical) has lighter touch and quicker decay."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "in-music",
      "title": "In music",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Solo repertoire from Bach to contemporary. Accompaniment for songs and céilí bands. Compositional tool — harmonic and contrapuntal possibilities in two hands. Cristofori solved hammer escapement; Steinway developed cross-stringing and duplex scaling."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
